{"author":{"id":"585844baa6074d44d241f46aa7c9f21ff46653adf617d7f070fbfe327626c989"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794949692,"nonce":"XcvOA67mh/q+vavGe4vemf+4K7Q=","message":"The available Linux NUC and local Safari have now been tested. The earlier test-tooling separation is committed as hxn-audio 0af6164 and hxn 62a8c83; those commits have not been pushed.\n\nLinux x86-64 NUC: the current hxn CLI was built from the authorized source snapshot in an isolated workspace. Rust workspace tests pass; the real-time/concurrency suite passed three repeats. Offline checks pass on stock HashLink 1.15, Ash 0.1.17 hybrid/JIT/interpreter, hxcpp and CPPIA. Packaged-library ALSA output passes on stock HashLink, all three Ash modes and hxcpp, including overlapping tones, streaming, pause/resume and default-output reopening. Backend estimates are approximately 18.2–18.6 ms. The native AAC fixture passes two serial repeats each on stock HashLink, Ash hybrid and hxcpp. Each run decodes/transfers/consumes 48,128 frames through the bounded ring and drains EOF. A direct GStreamer faad decode of the identical 48 AAC packets produces exactly the same 48,128 frames (192,512 bytes of mono f32), distinguishing the platform decoder output count from mixer transfer loss. Human audibility and acoustic loopback were not measured on the NUC.\n\nThe NUC needed a real pkgconf instead of an existing unrelated wrapper that omitted ALSA libraries, the ALSA development link, and GStreamer faad/libfaad. These were unpacked into the test prefix and selected through process-local environment variables, without changing system packages or global haxelib configuration. Its small /tmp tmpfs filled during hxcpp linking; only this workspace was moved onto disk. The stock-HashLink installed-package playback exposed a harness gap: its adapter directory was not on the dynamic loader search path. The test runner now supplies and restores that path, matching hxn's native launch behavior. Linux and macOS playback validate the adjustment.\n\nSafari 27.2 on the Mac: Ash wasm AAC playback passed before the fix, including a trusted activation, 49,152 frames and explicit completion. Unthreaded hxcpp reproducibly stalled in initialization. A new AudioContext was already suspended; the redundant awaited suspend request remained pending even after a trusted key resumed the context. Initializing an already-suspended context directly fixes the hang. The corrected source was packaged for Ash wasm and both hxcpp wasm modes. Safari then passed clean AAC playback on all three modes with trusted key activation, pause/resume, 49,152 transferred/consumed frames, EOF and an approximately 18.5 ms backend estimate. hxcpp repeats and the rebuilt Ash repeat passed. Safari WebDriver remote automation was disabled, so the browser was exercised through its UI without changing that setting. Ash uses ash serve; Emscripten uses emrun so its stdout/exit POST transport works. Serving Emscripten via a plain static server caused a diagnostic network exception after playback, resolved by using its proper test server. The owned Safari tab and temporary servers/driver were closed.\n\nThe new tests/browser-startup.mjs regression uses the actual DSP module and contexts whose redundant suspend never settles. It fails against the previous implementation and passes with the fix for both initially suspended and initially running contexts. Browser CI runs it. Fresh installed-package Chrome AAC checks pass twice on each of the three browser modes (six runs). DSP shared-memory-growth and mailbox-GC checks, Haxe formatter, skill validation, workflow YAML, Rust formatting, clippy with warnings denied and workspace tests pass. Design/conventions/skill/testing instructions are current. Temporary browser instrumentation was removed by the clean rebuild. The Safari fix, regression, harness loader-path correction and associated docs/CI updates are uncommitted; nothing was pushed or published.\n\nThese results cover Linux runtime/device progress and desktop Safari instead of deferring them to CI. Windows CI/audio-device validation, physical mobile-browser playback, Firefox coverage, real headset/route/interruption/focus transitions, acoustic loopback and release/integration remain separate outstanding work. Keep the parent open for the remaining validation/integration requirements.","files":null}]}